# Copper Linux boot entries.
#
# This is the single list of kernel command lines. A bootloader provider reads
# it and renders whatever its own menu syntax is; GRUB's provider lives in
# iso/bootloaders/grub/. Nothing in build.sh names a menu entry any more.
#
# Format, one entry per non-comment line:
#   id|menu title|kernel command line
#
# The console order matters more than it looks. The kernel registers every
# console= it is given, so printk goes to all of them, but /dev/console -- what
# init and everything after it write to -- is the LAST one listed. ttyS0 last
# once sent our own messages to a serial port a VM without one does not have,
# and the screen sat on "Booting up kernel" looking like a hang. tty0 is last.
#
# Flags on the default entry:
#   splash / plymouth.*  the Copper boot animation; nosplash turns it off
#   quiet loglevel=3     the screen belongs to the animation, then to systemd
#   systemd.show_status=1  without it the splash suppresses the [ OK ] lines
# The systemd entry adds copper.init=systemd so /init execs systemd instead of
# the busybox init. No switch on the default entry means /etc/copper-init (the
# wizard's saved answer) decides.
default|Copper Linux|splash plymouth.ignore-udev plymouth.ignore-serial-consoles quiet loglevel=3 systemd.show_status=1 console=ttyS0,115200 console=tty0
systemd|Copper Linux (systemd init)|splash plymouth.ignore-udev plymouth.ignore-serial-consoles quiet loglevel=3 systemd.show_status=1 copper.init=systemd console=ttyS0,115200 console=tty0
verbose|Copper Linux (verbose)|console=ttyS0,115200 console=tty0 panic=10
debug|Copper Linux (debug)|console=ttyS0,115200 console=tty0 ignore_loglevel loglevel=8 earlyprintk=serial,ttyS0,115200 panic=10
initramfs-shell|Copper Linux (initramfs shell)|console=ttyS0,115200 console=tty0 rdinit=/bin/busybox sh
